## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser read dates month first.** Every tenant writes the day first, so
"7 April" came out as "4 July". This is the bug #4471 named. Fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for
eu locales`.

**`hint`: booking-v3 told people to type the month first.** Its date field said `MM/DD/YYYY`.
People at day-first tenants on booking-v3 typed what the hint said; the form read it the tenant's
way, and the date flipped. The parser fix did not touch this. Won't fix: booking-v3 is retired.

**`one-day`: dates move by a day, not a month.** Tuesday shows as Monday; a client in Lisbon gets
Tuesday and one in Toronto gets Monday; an invite arrives before its booking. That is a time-zone
conversion, not a swapped day and month, so the parser fix does not reach it. The desk had noted
these reports (`desk-notes.json`) and filed them under `parser`.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but <code>one-day</code> is not fixed. Keep its ticket open, and send new
one-day reports there, not to a closed ticket.
</Callout>

## How the reports were placed

- **One id, one report.** The export pages overlap, so some reports come twice. They count once.
- **By tenant, not by intake's tag.** Intake guesses from the reporter's browser. The cause
  depends on the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`), so this page groups by that.
- **Counted where the desk filed them.** A report the desk read as a one-day move stays in the
  `parser` count, as on the #4471 page, and is also counted under `one-day` (`problems.ts`).

The numbers above are computed from the ticket export at render time; when the desk merges more
reports, the table follows.

